  "query_preview": "..\n\nCyclonic influence from the closed low circulation over Ontario has \nsupported an expansion of stratocu across the most of the northern \nhalf of SE MI this afternoon. This cloud quickly fades late this \nevening as troughing finally dislodges east allowing northern Ohio \nValley high pressure to fully build over southern lower MI tonight. \nCooler night is again in store albeit a couple degrees warmer than \nlast night (more mid 50s than the lower 50s yesterday). High \npressure drifts east over the course of Tuesday maintaining a \npleasant day with partly cloudy skies and highs back around 80. \n\nNext shortwave drops out of the Canadian Prairie into the northern \nGreat Lakes Tuesday night. Locally, this results in a developing \nwarm front lifting through the area early Wednesday morning. \nOrganizing low level southwest winds result in slow moisture \ntransport over the course of Wednesday as dewpoints climb from the \nupper 50s in the AM to the mid 60s by evening. Given the gradual \narrival of moisture and the main theta-e plume tied along the \napproaching cold frontal slope, expectation is the majority of the \nday will stay dry. Possible exception is the Tri-Cities/northern \nThumb who reside closer to the mid-level trough base that will be \nsinking through northern lower MI potentially offering a few passing \nafternoon showers. Instead, main window for showers/storms for the \nentire area is looking to between ~23-06Z coinciding with both the \narrival of the cold front/preceding pooling of theta-e as well as \nthe mid/upper trough digging south through the state. In terms of \nsevere potential, set-up remains marginal. Late evening arrival is \non the backedge of the diurnal cycle allowing for at least several \nhundred J/kg of MLCAPE being place; however column wind fields \naren't particularly robust, largely unidirectional 20-30kt flow \nyielding shear values between 25-30kts. Given this setup, the \ncurrent Day 3 Marginal Risk from SPC appears warranted for an \nisolated strong/borderline severe cell. \n\nWhile the cold front clears the region by Thursday morning, trailing \nairmass is essentially the same as dewpoints remain in the mid 60s \nwith highs in the lower 80s. Lapse rates steepen by afternoon as \nupper troughing settles over the central Great Lakes sparking \nchances scattered showers/thunderstorms. Passage of a secondary cold \nfront Thursday night should finally shunt the bulk of humidity south \nreturning dewpoints to upper 50s/60.",
